{{nihongo|'''Naoto Emi'''|江見 直人|Emi Naoto}} (10 October 1921 – 14 June 2009) was a Sanese politician who served as the 37th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from January 1982 to December 1987. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), Emi presided over the zenith of the [[Go-go 8090]] asset bubble and was the longest-serving prime minister of the 1980s boom era. His "Industrial Rationalization" policies helped transition Okaiken's economy from heavy manufacturing to high-tech electronics, fundamentally reshaping the nation's industrial base.

== Early life ==
Emi was born in [[Kizawa]], [[Mie Prefecture]], in 1921, the son of a textile factory manager. His childhood was marked by the economic hardships of the interwar period and the devastation of the [[Great Adonian War]], which destroyed his family's factory and forced them into temporary displacement. These experiences instilled in him a deep commitment to industrial modernization as the path to national resilience.

He attended the prefectural school in [[Mie (city)|Mie]], where his aptitude for mathematics drew the attention of his instructors. He went on to the [[Imperial University of Okami]], where he studied engineering with a focus on industrial processes. Unlike many of his political contemporaries who studied law or economics, Emi's technical background gave him a unique perspective on the relationship between technology, productivity, and economic growth. Fellow students later recalled his habit of sketching production-flow diagrams in the margins of his political theory texts.

After graduating in 1943, Emi worked briefly in the steel industry, taking a position as a process engineer at the Sasedo Iron & Steel works before entering politics. His early career was shaped by the reconstruction period, and he became convinced that the nation's future lay not in the heavy industries that had defined the Imperial era but in the emerging field of electronics and information technology.

== Political career ==
Emi was first elected to the House of Commoners in 1958, representing [[Mie 3rd District]]. He built his political career on a platform of industrial modernization, arguing that the nation's economic future depended on its ability to transition from resource-intensive heavy manufacturing to knowledge-intensive high-tech industries.

During the 1960s and 1970s, Emi served in a series of ministerial positions related to industry and technology. As Minister of Construction (1968–1970) he oversaw the early expressway expansion programs, and as Minister of International Trade and Industry (1974–1977) under the Nakasone administration, he championed the "Industrial Rationalization" program that provided tax incentives and government subsidies for companies investing in semiconductor fabrication, computer technology, and telecommunications infrastructure.

His advocacy for the high-tech sector was not universally popular. Traditional industrial interests—particularly the steel and shipbuilding lobbies—resisted the shift in government priorities, arguing that heavy manufacturing remained the backbone of the national economy. Emi countered that the resource constraints of the post-war era made heavy industry unsustainable and that the nation's competitive advantage lay in its highly educated workforce and technological innovation. The rivalry with the steel faction, led by Trade Council chairman Ubukata Tsunetomo, became one of the defining intraparty battles of the 1970s; Emi's victory over Ubukata in the 1981 LDP presidential contest cleared his path to the premiership.

== Premiership (1982–1987) ==
=== Industrial Rationalization ===
[[File:Emi Industrial Rationalization 1984.png|thumb|right|200px|Emi's Industrial Rationalization program accelerated the transition from heavy manufacturing to high-tech electronics]]
Emi took office on 17 January 1982, succeeding [[Junichiro Okazaki]] as the nation entered the most dynamic phase of the [[Go-go 8090]] economic boom. His premiership was defined by the "Industrial Rationalization" program, a comprehensive policy framework that accelerated the transition from heavy manufacturing to high-tech electronics.

The program had three components: first, targeted tax incentives for companies investing in research and development, semiconductor fabrication, and software engineering; second, government-funded research consortia that brought together universities, private companies, and national laboratories to develop next-generation technologies; and third, the establishment of special economic zones with reduced regulatory burdens for technology companies.

The results were dramatic. Between 1982 and 1987, the nation's semiconductor production capacity tripled, its share of the global electronics market grew from 12% to 28%, and the technology sector's contribution to GDP surpassed that of heavy manufacturing for the first time. The "Sanese Miracle" of the 1980s was, in large measure, an Emi creation.

=== The asset bubble ===
[[File:Go-go 8090 OSE 225 Chart 1987.png|thumb|right|200px|The OSE 225 stock index tripled during Emi's tenure, fueled by speculative investment]]
Emi's premiership also coincided with the inflation of the [[Go-go 8090]] asset bubble. The financial deregulation initiated by the Okazaki administration, combined with the Emi government's expansionary monetary policy, created a flood of cheap credit that fueled speculative investment in real estate and equities. The OSE 225 stock index tripled during Emi's tenure, and land prices in central Okami reached levels that made the city the most expensive real estate market in the world.

Emi's government did little to restrain the speculative excess. His "Doubling of National Assets" plan, which encouraged private investment while using tax revenues to expand the national pension and healthcare systems, was enormously popular but contributed to the belief that asset prices would continue to rise indefinitely. The Bank of Okaiken, under pressure from the Finance Ministry, kept interest rates low despite clear signs of overheating.

=== Foreign policy ===
Emi's foreign policy achievements included the normalization of diplomatic relations with the [[Sansi|People's Republic of Sansi]] in 1982 and the signing of the Joint Declaration with [[Moselle]] in 1983, which deepened the economic and security cooperation established by the Mosellan Trade Accord. His government also oversaw the nation's growing engagement with the [[Adonian Community]], particularly in the areas of development assistance and peacekeeping operations.

=== Domestic policy beyond Industrial Rationalization ===
Beyond the Industrial Rationalization program, the Emi government pursued several significant domestic policy initiatives. The government introduced the "Doubling of National Assets" plan in 1983, which combined tax incentives for private investment with expanded government spending on social programs. The plan was enormously popular, as it promised to increase national wealth while simultaneously expanding the social safety net. However, the plan contributed to the growing fiscal imbalance and the inflation of asset prices that would characterize the late 1980s.

The government also introduced the Urban Development Act of 1984, which authorized the construction of new commercial and residential districts in the major cities and provided tax incentives for private developers. The Act was a response to the growing demand for office space and housing in the urban centers, and it contributed to the dramatic expansion of the construction industry during the Go-go 8090 era. The Act was also criticized for contributing to the real estate speculation that inflated the asset bubble.

On the social policy front, the Emi government introduced the National Healthcare Reform Act of 1985, which expanded the national health insurance system and established a standardized benefits package. The reform was a significant expansion of the social safety net, and it was widely popular despite fiscal conservatives' usual aversion to new spending programs. The government also introduced the Elder Care Act of 1986, which established a national long-term care insurance program for the elderly—a response to the nation's rapidly aging population.

=== Foreign policy beyond the Sansian normalization ===
Beyond the normalization of relations with Sansi, the Emi government pursued several significant foreign policy initiatives. The government deepened Okaiken's economic integration with the Adonian Community, supporting the organization's efforts to establish a single market among member states. The government also negotiated the Adonian Community Technology Cooperation Agreement of 1984, which provided for joint research projects and the exchange of scientific personnel among member states.

The Mosellan relationship was deepened through the Joint Declaration of 1983, which expanded the economic and security cooperation established by the Mosellan Trade Accord. The Declaration provided for the coordination of monetary policy, the establishment of a joint economic commission, and the expansion of cultural and educational exchanges. The Declaration was a significant step toward the full economic integration that would later be achieved through the Adonian Community Single Market.

In the Shendan, the Emi government expanded the Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative, negotiating bilateral investment agreements with several Shendan nations and providing increased development assistance through the Sanese International Cooperation Organization (SICO). The government also authorized the establishment of the Shendan Maritime Security Initiative, which provided for the coordination of naval patrols and anti-piracy operations in the region. The Initiative was a response to the growing threat of piracy in the southern Shendan, which threatened Okaiken's commercial shipping lanes.

=== The Emi Cabinet ===
Emi's first cabinet was a coalition of the LDP, the Okaiken New Party (ONP), and the New Democratic Party (NDT), mirroring the Okazaki coalition. Foreign Minister Hiyama Tsuneho conducted the Sansi normalization talks and negotiated the Mosellan Joint Declaration. Finance Minister Kakizaki Ryōsuke, a veteran of the Budget Committee, managed the low-interest policy coordination with the Bank of Okaiken and became the cabinet's most vocal internal advocate for tightening before the bubble grew further—advice that Emi, wary of stalling the boom that underpinned his program's popularity, declined to take. Chief Cabinet Secretary Masuhara Kiyonori served as the government's chief spokesman throughout the premiership.

Following the 1986 general election, Emi reshuffled the cabinet twice, dropping the NDT and bringing younger LDP figures into the key economic ministries. The post-1986 cabinet included future party president Masazumi Katō as Finance Minister, positioning him as the heir apparent.

=== Resignation ===
In the autumn of 1987, with the LDP presidential term expiring and the economy at full boil, Emi announced that he would not seek a third term as party president and would hand over the premiership to his successor. Publicly, he cited the party's tradition of two-term presidencies and his belief that "the second stage of the miracle requires new hands." Privately, associates later revealed that Emi had grown uneasy with the speculative excesses of the late boom—Kakizaki's warnings had left their mark—but calculated that a leadership transition was preferable to presiding over the correction himself.

He resigned on 1 December 1987 and was succeeded by [[Masazumi Kato|Masazumi Katō]], whose government inherited the bubble at its absolute peak. Historians have debated ever since whether Emi's exit was prescience or good timing; his own memoir offers support for both readings.

== Post-premiership ==
Emi retired from the House of Commoners at the end of his term in December 1987, declining a seat in the House of Officers that the party offered as a customary honor. He withdrew almost entirely from public life, making only rare appearances at LDP anniversary events and at ceremonies for the research consortia he had founded.

In retirement he wrote two volumes of memoirs, ''Blueprint of a Miracle'' (1994) and ''The Engineer's Cabinet'' (2001), which remain primary sources for the study of the Go-go 8090 era. He also served as honorary chairman of the Okami Institute of Technology from 1990 until his death. In his later years he became an unexpected advocate for financial literacy education, telling a university audience in 2003 that "a nation that believes asset prices only rise is a nation that has forgotten engineering."

Emi died of heart failure at his home in [[Okami]] on 14 June 2009 at the age of 87. His official memorial service, held at the Okami Metropolitan Assembly Hall, was attended by Emperor Masahito and four serving and former prime ministers. In a widely noted gesture, Masazumi Katō—by then himself a retired elder statesman—delivered the eulogy, defending his predecessor against the charge that the bubble was an Emi creation: "He built the engine. The rest of us poured in too much fuel."

== Personal life ==
Emi married Fujiko Ōtomo, a schoolteacher from Mie Prefecture, in 1948. The couple had three children: Takeo, who became a process engineer at a semiconductor fabrication firm in the Suzuka region; Mariko, a physician; and Naoki, a university lecturer in economics. Emi was known among his staff for his habit of carrying a slide rule long after electronic calculators made it obsolete, reportedly telling aides that "the slide rule never lies about orders of magnitude."

== Electoral history ==
{{see also|Okaiken general election, 1982|Okaiken general election, 1983|Okaiken general election, 1986}}
Emi represented [[Mie 3rd District]] continuously from 1958 until his retirement in 1987, winning twelve consecutive elections. As party leader he led the LDP to victories in the general elections of 1982, 1983, and 1986, the last of which produced the largest governing majority of the Go-go 8090 era.

