== Legacy ==
Emi is one of the most consequential and controversial prime ministers of the post-war era. His Industrial Rationalization program successfully transformed the nation's industrial base and laid the foundation for its dominance in the global electronics market. The "Sanese Miracle" of the 1980s—the combination of rapid economic growth, technological innovation, and rising living standards—is largely attributed to his policies.

However, Emi is also criticized for his failure to address the growing asset bubble. His government's expansionary monetary policy and its reluctance to impose regulatory constraints on speculative investment contributed to the inflation of asset prices to unsustainable levels. When the bubble burst in 1989, the resulting economic crisis would define the next decade of Sanese politics.

Emi himself acknowledged this criticism in his later years, writing in his memoirs that "we were so focused on building the future that we failed to see the foundations cracking beneath us."

Public assessments of Emi have oscillated with the economy. During the Lost Decade of the 1990s he was routinely ranked among the most culpable figures of the bubble era; as the technology sector he championed became the backbone of the 21st-century economy, retrospective rankings of prime ministers began placing him in the top tier. A 2024 ''Okami Times'' survey of historians ranked Emi the third-greatest post-war prime minister, behind only [[Kenzan Mihashi]] among pre-war figures and first among post-war premiers.

